UPDATE public.information
SET
  content_text = '<h1>PART 1 — KEY LESSONS (OUTLINE)</h1>
<h2>1. Less but better</h2>
<ul><li>Essentialism is the disciplined pursuit of fewer, higher-value activities.</li><li>Doing everything modestly well is inferior to doing the vital few excellently.</li></ul>
<h2>2. You choose — or someone else will</h2>
<ul><li>Tradeoffs are real; denying them hands your calendar to other people’s priorities.</li><li>Saying yes to everything is saying no to your highest contribution.</li></ul>
<h2>3. Explore before you commit</h2>
<ul><li>Create space to discern what matters: escape, play, sleep, and deep looking.</li><li>Clarity requires margin; burnout clouds judgment.</li></ul>
<h2>4. Eliminate ruthlessly</h2>
<ul><li>Cut, edit, and uncommit from the trivial many.</li><li>Ask reverse questions: if I didn’t already have this, how hard would I fight to get it?</li></ul>
<h2>5. Execute with buffers and routines</h2>
<ul><li>Remove friction, build buffers, and make the essential the easy default.</li><li>Small, consistent wins beat heroic last-minute efforts.</li></ul>
<h2>6. Protect the asset — you</h2>
<ul><li>Sleep, play, and recovery are not rewards after work; they are inputs to wise choice.</li><li>An exhausted mind cannot tell essential from noise.</li></ul>

<h1>PART 2 — ESSENTIALISM</h1>
<h2>A 26-Minute Read Summary</h2>

<h3>Chapter 1: The Way of the Essentialist</h3>
<p>Greg McKeown’s Essentialism begins with a simple diagnosis: modern life rewards the appearance of busyness. We say yes to please, stay late to look committed, and fill calendars until every hour has a meeting. The result is the paradox of success — more options arrive as you do well, and those options become distractions that dilute the work that made you successful.</p>
<p>Essentialism answers with a mindset: less but better. It is not laziness, and it is not minimalism as aesthetic. It is a disciplined operating system for choosing the vital few contributions over the trivial many. Non-essentialists ask, “How can I fit it all in?” Essentialists ask, “What is the most important thing I should be doing with my time and energy right now?”</p>
<p>The book’s emotional honesty matters. Saying no feels socially risky. Clearing commitments feels like loss. McKeown reframes that discomfort as the price of a meaningful life. If everything is important, nothing is. If you refuse to choose, the market, your inbox, and other people’s urgency will choose for you — usually poorly.</p>
<p>Start today by listing everything competing for your attention this week. Circle only the few items that truly advance your highest goals or deepest values. Everything else is a candidate for decline, delay, or deletion. That single sorting habit is the gateway to the rest of the method.</p>
<p>McKeown distinguishes Essentialism from efficiency. Efficiency asks how to do more per hour. Essentialism asks whether the hour should exist. You can become devastatingly efficient at non-essential work and still miss your life. The essentialist’s first victory is not a faster email triage; it is a clearer aim.</p>
<p>Watch for the social rewards of non-essentialism. Busyness signals importance in many workplaces. Availability signals niceness in many friend groups. If your identity is entangled with being the person who never declines, Essentialism will feel like ego death before it feels like freedom. Expect that discomfort. It often means the medicine is working.</p>
<p>A simple starter vow: ‘I will not trade my highest contribution for immediate social comfort this week.’ Put it where you see it. Essentialism begins as a decision about identity before it becomes a calendar technique. Without that decision, tools collapse under the first urgent request.</p>
<p>Keep a visible ‘not now’ list to reduce the mental load of declining. Your brain relaxes when it trusts that good ideas are parked rather than erased. Essentialism is selective, not amnesiac. Review the list monthly; promote only what still scores near ninety.</p>
<p><strong>Lesson:</strong> Essentialism means deliberately choosing less but better so your energy goes to what matters most.</p>

<h3>Chapter 2: Choice, Tradeoffs, and the Myth of “Both”</h3>
<p>Non-essentialists hate tradeoffs. They try to have both: the promotion and the side projects, the social yes and the deep work, the perfect parenting schedule and the always-on career. Essentialists accept that every yes contains a no. The question is whether you make the tradeoff consciously.</p>
<p>McKeown argues that tradeoffs are not failures of ambition; they are the physics of finite time. When you pretend otherwise, you pay in quality, relationships, and health. Leaders who refuse tradeoffs create teams that thrash between shifting priorities. Individuals who refuse tradeoffs become resentful — busy, but unclear why their life feels thin.</p>
<p>A practical tool is to replace “Can I do both?” with “Which problem do I want?” or “What can I go big on?” Choosing implies loss, and naming the loss reduces vague guilt. If you choose deep work over a networking event, write the reason. If you choose family dinner over another optional call, treat it as strategy, not selfishness.</p>
<p>Also watch for fake tradeoffs sold by hustle culture — the idea that sleep is optional or that boundaries equal lack of drive. Essentialism says the opposite: boundaries protect the capacity required for excellence. Ambition without tradeoffs is noise. Ambition with tradeoffs becomes a career and a life.</p>
<p>Leaders who deny tradeoffs create thrash: five ‘top priorities,’ constant context switching, and teams that never finish. Essentialist leaders name the priority that wins when conflicts appear. They accept that some good ideas will wait. Paradoxically, shipping the vital few builds more trust than performing commitment to everything.</p>
<p>Personally, try a tradeoff journal for two weeks. Each day, record one choice where you consciously picked A over B and why. Patterns will appear: maybe you always sacrifice health for urgency, or deep work for messages. Awareness lets you rebalance. Tradeoffs stop being accidental losses and become designed investments.</p>
<p>Try ‘tradeoff conversations’ at work. When asked to take new scope, reply with options: ‘I can do A by Friday if B moves to next week — which matters more?’ You force the organization to be essentialist with you instead of absorbing infinite load silently.</p>
<p>In personal life, tradeoffs include attention to phones versus people in the room. Announce the tradeoff out loud at dinner if needed: ‘I am choosing you over the screen for the next hour.’ Naming it teaches children Essentialism more effectively than a lecture.</p>
<p><strong>Lesson:</strong> Accept tradeoffs consciously — if you do not choose, other people’s priorities will choose for you.</p>

<h3>Chapter 3: Explore — Create Space to See Clearly</h3>
<p>You cannot know what is essential if you never step back. McKeown’s first major phase is explore: create space to look, listen, and think. In a hyper-connected world, exploration requires deliberate escape from the noise — time without inbox, meetings, or constant input.</p>
<p>Exploration is not endless research as procrastination. It is focused curiosity: reading widely enough to spot patterns, talking to people who challenge your assumptions, and observing where your talents meet real need. Essentialists explore more options than non-essentialists at the start so they can commit to fewer options at the end.</p>
<p>Schedule exploration like work. A weekly quiet hour, a quarterly half-day strategy walk, or a device-free morning can reveal that half your commitments no longer match your goals. Use questions: If I could do only one thing this quarter, what would it be? What would I stop if I were ten times more selective?</p>
<p>Without exploration, elimination becomes random cutting, and execution becomes efficient motion in the wrong direction. Protect thinking time as fiercely as delivery time. Clarity is a competitive advantage precisely because so few people leave room for it.</p>
<p>Exploration includes listening to your own envy and energy. Notice which problems make you lean forward and which commitments make you heavy before you begin. Those signals are data for selection later. Also explore discontinuities: industry shifts, family seasons, and skills you have outgrown. Essentials change as life chapters change.</p>
<p>Protect exploration on the calendar with a name so others respect it: ‘thinking block,’ ‘strategy walk,’ ‘research morning.’ If it remains unnamed leftover time, it will be eaten. McKeown’s escape is not permanent retreat from responsibility; it is temporary distance so responsibility can be aimed.</p>
<p>Exploration fails when it becomes endless browsing. Time-box research. End every exploration block with a provisional priority statement. Essentialists explore to decide, not to delay deciding forever. Curiosity is fuel; commitment is the engine.</p>
<p><strong>Lesson:</strong> Build regular space to explore and think — clarity about the essential requires margin from the noise.</p>

<h3>Chapter 4: Play, Sleep, and the Conditions for Insight</h3>
<p>Essentialism surprises some readers by elevating play and sleep. McKeown treats them as strategic assets for discernment, not as soft rewards after productivity. Play sparks exploration and novel connections. Sleep restores the cognitive horsepower required to distinguish vital work from busywork.</p>
<p>Chronic sleep debt makes everything feel urgent and nothing feel clear. You say yes because you lack the energy to negotiate. You confuse activity with progress. Play, likewise, is not childish escape; it is a mode of mind that loosens rigid thinking and reveals better options. Teams that never play often stagnate in the same tired solutions.</p>
<p>Protect sleep as a non-negotiable system: consistent bedtime, reduced late screens, and a refusal to treat exhaustion as a badge. Insert small play: walks without podcasts, hobbies without KPIs, humor in meetings. These are not anti-ambition; they are how ambition stays intelligent.</p>
<p>If your culture glorifies burnout, practice quiet noncompliance. You cannot out-essentialize biology. The essentialist’s edge is judgment, and judgment is a rested, playful mind applied to hard choices.</p>
<p>Organizations that treat sleep as weakness pay in bad decisions, safety incidents, and shallow work dressed up as grit. If you manage people, stop glorifying midnight emails. Reward judgment and outcomes. If you are an individual contributor, negotiate outcomes-based evaluation so rest is compatible with excellence.</p>
<p>Play can be tiny. Five minutes of improvisation, a curious conversation unrelated to KPIs, or sketching an idea by hand can unlock a stuck project more effectively than another forced hour of grinding. Essentialists are not anti-work; they are anti-stupid-work produced by depleted brains.</p>
<p>If you cannot sleep longer tonight, protect sleep regularity and a wind-down ritual as a first step. Perfect rest is rare; better rest is available. Pair it with one playful micro-habit that does not require gear or planning — doodling, music, a silly voice note to a friend — to keep play from becoming another optimized chore.</p>
<p><strong>Lesson:</strong> Treat sleep and play as tools for better judgment — exhausted minds cannot reliably spot what is essential.</p>

<h3>Chapter 5: Select — The 90 Percent Rule</h3>
<p>Once you have explored, you must select. McKeown offers a tough filter often summarized as the 90 percent rule: if you are not ranking an opportunity as at least a 90 out of 100 against your criteria, treat it as a zero and say no. The middle zone — the 60s and 70s — is where careers get cluttered.</p>
<p>Define explicit criteria before you evaluate options. For a project: Does it advance my primary goal? Am I uniquely positioned to contribute? What is the opportunity cost? Emotional criteria alone — guilt, flattery, fear of missing out — will keep you trapped in the non-essential.</p>
<p>Practice selective yeses. A clear yes to a vital project should feel energizing even when hard. A reluctant yes often signals a future resentment. When unsure, buy time: “Let me check my commitments and reply tomorrow.” Speed-yes is a non-essentialist habit; deliberate reply is an essentialist one.</p>
<p>Apply the same filter to existing work, not only new invites. Many calendars are museums of past yeses. Re-score every major commitment against today’s criteria. What passed at 70 last year may fail at 90 now — and that is progress, not inconsistency.</p>
<p>Criteria should be written before temptation arrives. When an opportunity is flattering, your standards soften. Pre-commit: ‘I only take projects that hit these three thresholds.’ Share criteria with a partner who will hold you honest. Selection is easier when your future self has already voted.</p>
<p>Apply the 90 percent rule to possessions and information diets too. Feeds, newsletters, and unfinished gadgets can be non-essential clutter in different clothing. Raise the bar for what enters your home and your head. Attention is the scarcest asset Essentialism protects.</p>
<p>Create an opportunity parking lot. Not every non-yes must disappear from memory; some belong on a dated list for later re-evaluation. Parking lots prevent FOMO-driven yeses while honoring that timing changes essentials. Revisit quarterly with fresh criteria.</p>
<p>If a senior leader pressures a yes, escalate the tradeoff rather than silently accepting overload. Essentialists make the cost visible. Organizations improve when someone refuses to pretend capacity is infinite. That courage is contagious when paired with respect and options.</p>
<p><strong>Lesson:</strong> Use strict selection criteria — if it is not a clear yes against what matters, make it a no.</p>

<h3>Chapter 6: Eliminate — Edit Your Life Like a Pro</h3>
<p>Elimination is the emotional core of Essentialism. Editing hurts because every commitment once seemed justified. McKeown encourages you to become an editor of your life: cut words, projects, and obligations that do not earn their place. Great editors are not destroyers; they are servants of clarity.</p>
<p>A powerful question for sunk-cost commitments: If I did not already have this obligation, how much would I sacrifice to get it? If the answer is “not much,” begin an exit plan. Another tool is zero-based commitment: imagine rebuilding your calendar from scratch — what would you put back?</p>
<p>Elimination includes social courage. You will disappoint people. Essentialists learn graceful nos: appreciation, clear decline, and when appropriate a helpful alternative. “I can’t take this on and still deliver on X” is honest. Vague maybes are crueler in the long run because they waste everyone’s time.</p>
<p>Start with visible clutter: meetings without agendas, recurring reports nobody reads, committees that exist from habit. Freeing even a few hours creates oxygen for the essential. Editing is not a one-time purge; it is a recurring practice as new non-essentials creep back in.</p>
<p>Editing also applies to identity roles. Maybe you became ‘the fixer’ or ‘the volunteer.’ Those roles can be honorable and still overcrowded. Essentialism invites role pruning: keep the roles that express your highest contribution, and release roles held only by guilt. Talk openly with stakeholders so exits do not feel like sudden abandonment.</p>
<p>Use a reverse pilot: pause a recurring meeting or commitment for two weeks and observe whether value truly drops. Many ‘essential’ rituals survive only by inertia. If nobody notices the pause, you found waste. If pain appears, you learned what to redesign rather than merely endure.</p>
<p>Edit digital life with the same ruthlessness: unfollow, unsubscribe, archive, delete. Non-essential inputs create non-essential impulses. A calmer information diet makes elimination of commitments easier because fewer random desires are being injected hourly.</p>
<p>After a big elimination pass, expect emptiness or guilt. Sit with it. Non-essentialists refill the void immediately with new busywork. Essentialists let white space remain long enough for the vital few to breathe. Boredom in the first week after decluttering is often a sign of healing attention.</p>
<p><strong>Lesson:</strong> Ruthlessly edit commitments — ask how hard you’d fight to get what you already have, and cut what fails the test.</p>

<h3>Chapter 7: The Courage to Say No</h3>
<p>Saying no is a skill. McKeown normalizes awkwardness and offers tactics: separate the decision from the relationship, delay your answer, give a clear reason tied to priorities, and avoid long justifications that invite negotiation. A short, respectful no is kinder than a slow, resentful yes.</p>
<p>Internal barriers matter as much as external pressure. People-pleasing, fear of missing out, and identity fused with being “helpful” all make refusal feel like self-betrayal. Reframe: every no to the non-essential is a yes to your contribution. You are not rejecting the person; you are protecting the work and the people already depending on you.</p>
<p>Role-play difficult nos. Prepare phrases for common scenarios: volunteer requests, scope creep, social invites, and “quick favors” that are not quick. If you lead, model no publicly so your team learns that boundaries are professional, not rebellious.</p>
<p>Track what happens after you say no. Often the sky does not fall. Reputation for reliability usually rises when you stop overpromising. Essentialism’s social lesson is that respect follows clarity more often than it follows constant availability.</p>
<p>Graceful nos often include a bridge: a referral, a later date, or a smaller alternative that you can actually deliver. Bridges preserve relationships without smuggling a full yes back in. Be careful, though — a bridge that still overloads you is a disguised non-essential commitment.</p>
<p>If saying no triggers anxiety, pair the skill with body practice: breathe, feel your feet, and remember your chosen priority. Social fear is physical. Essentialism fails when it stays intellectual while your nervous system keeps people-pleasing on autopilot.</p>
<p>When stakes are high, practice the no aloud before the meeting. Muscle memory reduces freeze. Also decide your ‘minimum polite warmth’ so you do not over-explain. Excess explanation is often a hidden request for permission to decline — permission you do not need.</p>
<p><strong>Lesson:</strong> Practice clear, respectful nos — protecting your highest contribution is not the same as rejecting people.</p>

<h3>Chapter 8: Uncommit and Escape Sunk Costs</h3>
<p>Even after you decide something is non-essential, exiting can be hard. Sunk costs — time already spent, reputation invested, money paid — trick the brain into continuing. Essentialists learn to uncommit: cancel gracefully, hand off, or sunset projects that no longer deserve oxygen.</p>
<p>Make uncommitment a process. List active projects. Mark those that would not pass today’s 90 percent test. For each, choose: finish with a hard deadline, delegate, pause, or stop. Communicate early. People can adapt to a clean exit; they struggle with endless half-attention.</p>
<p>Emotionally, expect grief. Ending a project can feel like ending a version of yourself. Name the loss, extract the learning, and move the freed capacity to the essential. Leaders should reward smart stops, not only launches. Cultures that punish course-correction trap everyone in zombie work.</p>
<p>A weekly uncommitment review keeps your portfolio honest. Ask: What am I doing only because I already started? That question alone can reclaim a life.</p>
<p>Uncommitment is easier with financial and reputational buffers. People stay in dead projects because they fear looking inconsistent. Reframe consistency as fidelity to purpose, not fidelity to old plans. Intelligent quitting is a leadership skill. Teach your team the difference between flaking and stewarding attention.</p>
<p>Create an ‘exit script’ template: appreciation, decision, reason tied to priorities, transition plan, and thanks. Rehearse it. The clearer the exit, the less drama. Sunk costs lose power when the path out is prewritten.</p>
<p>Finance uncommitment too: know cancellation fees and sunk versus recoverable costs. Sometimes paying a small fee to exit is the essentialist bargain compared with months of attention debt. Calculate attention cost in hours, not only money.</p>
<p><strong>Lesson:</strong> Exit gracefully from sunk-cost commitments so your future hours are not held hostage by past decisions.</p>

<h3>Chapter 9: Execute — Make the Essential Effortless</h3>
<p>McKeown’s third phase is execute. Once you know the essential, remove obstacles so progress becomes the path of least resistance. Non-essentialists rely on heroic effort and adrenaline. Essentialists build systems: routines, checklists, visible priorities, and environments that make the right action easy.</p>
<p>Identify friction. If writing is essential, prep the outline the night before and block the first morning hour. If health is essential, lay out clothes and schedule workouts like meetings. If a relationship is essential, put recurring time on the calendar instead of hoping spontaneity survives a packed week.</p>
<p>Execution also means focus on the present priority rather than thrashing among twenty. Win the next small milestone. Celebrate progress that reinforces identity as someone who follows through on the vital few. Momentum on the essential beats scattered motion across the trivial many.</p>
<p>Review weekly: Did my time match my stated essentials? If not, fix the system, not only your mood. Essentialism fails when it stays philosophical. It works when your calendar, desktop, and habits are edited to match your words.</p>
<p>Environment design beats motivation. Put the essential tool in reach and the distraction one click further away. If writing matters, open the document automatically at 8:30. If training matters, sleep in gym clothes if that is what it takes. Essentialists respect human frailty and design around it.</p>
<p>Visual management helps teams execute: a board with only three essential outcomes for the week beats a backlog of fifty equally loud tickets. Make the vital few impossible to ignore. When everything is pinned as urgent, Essentialism has already lost.</p>
<p>Batch the non-essential remnants you cannot yet eliminate — admin, messages — into tight windows so they cannot fragment essential execution. Batching is a truce with reality, not a betrayal of the philosophy. Pure worlds are rare; designed compromise is adult Essentialism.</p>
<p>End execution days with a shutdown phrase that lists unfinished essentials and parks them for tomorrow. Without shutdown, essentials leak into anxious evenings. McKeown’s system needs psychological closure as much as calendars. Closure protects sleep, which protects tomorrow’s discernment.</p>
<p><strong>Lesson:</strong> Execute by removing friction and building routines so the essential becomes the easy default.</p>

<h3>Chapter 10: Buffer, Subtract, and Prepare for Reality</h3>
<p>Life is unpredictable. Non-essentialists plan as if everything will go perfectly, then live in chronic emergency. Essentialists build buffers — extra time, money, and contingency — so surprises do not destroy the vital few. A buffer is not laziness; it is respect for reality.</p>
<p>Practically, estimate how long work will take, then add margin. Leave travel gaps between meetings. Keep a financial reserve so a minor shock does not force desperate choices. Protect white space in the week for spillover and thinking. When you subtract non-essentials, you create the raw material for buffers.</p>
<p>Also subtract from processes. Many workflows are overbuilt. Ask what you can remove and still deliver quality. Subtracting steps, approvals, and features often improves outcomes while freeing capacity. Essential execution is as much about deletion as addition.</p>
<p>Use extreme preparation for high-stakes essentials: pack early, rehearse talks, draft agendas. Preparation reduces last-minute thrash that invites non-essential firefighting. The calm essentialist is usually the prepared essentialist.</p>
<p>Buffers include relational honesty. Tell collaborators realistic timelines instead of fantasy dates that create future emergencies. Under-promising with integrity creates the slack Essentialism needs. Chronic overpromising is a non-essentialist addiction to short-term approval.</p>
<p>Subtracting can feel like loss of sophistication. Fancy processes signal seriousness in some cultures. Challenge that aesthetic. A simple checklist that people use beats an elaborate workflow people bypass. Essential execution prefers ugly-useful over pretty-ignored.</p>
<p>Build a ‘surprise fund’ of hours each week that is intentionally empty. When the surprise arrives, the essential does not get stolen. When no surprise arrives, use the fund for exploration or rest — both essentialist uses.</p>
<p><strong>Lesson:</strong> Build buffers and subtract unnecessary complexity so surprises do not crowd out what matters.</p>

<h3>Chapter 11: Progress — Small Wins on the Vital Few</h3>
<p>McKeown emphasizes small wins. Huge, vague goals encourage procrastination and heroics. Tiny, visible progress on an essential project builds motivation and learning. Non-essentialists wait for big blocks of time that never arrive. Essentialists advance in imperfect conditions with focused increments.</p>
<p>Define the “minimum viable progress” for your top priority each day. One meaningful page, one sales conversation, one resolved blocker. Track streaks of showing up for the essential. When interruptions hit, shrink the win rather than abandoning the priority.</p>
<p>Share progress with accountability partners or teams. Visibility creates healthy pressure and clarifies what “done” means. Beware of fake wins on non-essentials — inbox zero can feel productive while your vital project starves. Measure what you say matters.</p>
<p>Over months, small wins compound into disproportionate results — the “less but better” payoff. Essentialism is patient ambition: fewer targets, deeper investment, steadier execution.</p>
<p>Small wins should be tied to the essential goal, not to random productivity theater. Define leading indicators: pages toward the book, customer conversations toward the launch, recovery sessions toward the race. Review them weekly. If leading indicators stall, intervene early while stakes are small.</p>
<p>Beware streak obsession that ignores quality. A shrunk win still needs to be real. Essentialism is not gamified busyness; it is honest progress on the work that matters. Adjust win size with seasons of life so the system survives childbirth, illness, or peak job demands.</p>
<p>Stack small wins to identity: after each essential win, say ‘I keep promises to my priorities.’ This is not fluff; it counters the identity of the overcommitted helper. Identity language makes the next no cheaper.</p>
<p><strong>Lesson:</strong> Advance the essential with small, consistent wins instead of waiting for heroic stretches of perfect time.</p>

<h3>Chapter 12: Living Essentialism as a Daily Discipline</h3>
<p>Essentialism is not a weekend declutter; it is a way of operating. McKeown closes by inviting a lifestyle: regularly explore, eliminate, and execute. Expect relapse. Inboxes refill. Flattery returns. Success breeds new invitations. Without ritual, non-essentialism creeps back.</p>
<p>Install simple rituals. Daily: identify the one essential win. Weekly: review calendar against priorities and uncommit something. Quarterly: escape for deeper strategy and life design. Pair rituals with identity language: “I am someone who protects the vital few.” Identity makes hard nos easier.</p>
<p>Teach the people around you how to work with you. Publish your priorities. Set response-time expectations. Invite others to challenge your non-essentials. When teams share essentialist norms, everyone wastes less life in fake urgency.</p>
<p>Finally, remember the point. Less but better is not about colder efficiency; it is about a higher contribution and a saner life. Choose the vital few, and give them the respect of your full attention.</p>
<p>Living Essentialism means accepting that some people will misunderstand you. They may call you selfish or less ambitious. Results over years become your defense: deeper relationships, better work, fewer regrets. Keep a shortlist of why you chose this path and reread it when social pressure spikes.</p>
<p>End each week with three questions: What was essential? What non-essential slipped in? What will I eliminate next week? That ritual is the whole book in miniature. Less but better becomes true only as often as you practice it.</p>
<p>Teach Essentialism at home with shared calendars and a weekly family priority conversation. Households drown in logistics when everything is urgent. One family essential per week — a meal, a walk, a planning hour — trains the discipline in the place life actually happens.</p>
<p>Re-read your calendar every Sunday as a moral document: it reveals what you truly worship. If it does not match your stated essentials, revise without drama. Essentialism is less a personality and more a weekly honesty practice about time.</p>
<p><strong>Lesson:</strong> Make Essentialism a repeating discipline of explore, eliminate, and execute — so your days match your real priorities.</p>',
  duration_minutes = 26,
  cover_image_url = 'https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/isbn/9780804137386-L.jpg',
  short_description = 'Greg McKeown’s disciplined pursuit of less but better — explore, eliminate, execute, and protect the essential with sleep, play, and tradeoffs.',
